    "applicationCategory": "CalculatorApplication",
    "operatingSystem": "Web Browser",
    "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" }
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the mathematical formula for countersink plunge depth?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The plunge depth (h) for a conical countersink bit with included angle θ into a pre-drilled pilot hole of diameter d to achieve an outer chamfer diameter D is: h = (D - d) / (2 * tan(θ / 2)). For standard 82-degree US screws, tan(41°) is 0.8693, making depth h ≈ 0.575 * (D - d). For 90-degree metric screws, tan(45°) is 1.0, making depth h = 0.5 * (D - d)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between an 82-degree and a 90-degree countersink angle?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "An 82-degree countersink angle is the American National Standard (ASME/ANSI B18.6.1) used for virtually all standard US imperial wood screws, drywall screws, and machine screws. A 90-degree countersink is the European/ISO (DIN 7991 / ISO 10642) standard for metric fasteners. Using an 82-degree screw in a 90-degree countersunk hole creates a point-contact ridge along the top edge, preventing the screw head from seating flush."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why do wood screws require both a pilot hole and a shank clearance hole?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A pilot hole matches the root (minor) diameter of the screw threads so the core enters without bursting wood fibers. A shank clearance hole matches or slightly exceeds the screw's unthreaded outer shank diameter in the top board. Without a clearance hole, the threads grip both boards simultaneously, creating screw jacking where the joint cannot pull tight."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

