  <!-- Deep Editorial & Technical Documentation -->
  <div class="prose max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The Geometry of Countersinking: Achieving Perfect Flush Joinery</h2>
    <p>
      In fine woodworking, architectural cabinetry, and mechanical assembly, driving a flat-head wood screw flush with the material surface requires balancing three interdependent drilling operations: the <strong>pilot hole</strong>, the <strong>shank clearance hole</strong>, and the <strong>countersink chamfer</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose my-4">
      <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-4">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-amber-950 text-sm mb-1 flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span>📐</span> The Mathematical Countersink Formula
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-amber-900 leading-relaxed font-mono mb-2">
          Depth h = (D - d) / (2 × tan(θ / 2))
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-amber-900 leading-relaxed">
          Where <code>D</code> is the target outer chamfer diameter, <code>d</code> is the pre-drilled pilot hole diameter, and <code>θ</code> is the countersink tool's included angle. If no pilot hole is pre-drilled (or if drilling into solid stock), <code>d = 0</code> and the full conical depth is <code>D / (2 × tan(θ/2))</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-blue-950 text-sm mb-1 flex items-center gap-1.5">
          <span>⚙️</span> The "Screw Jacking" Hazard
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-blue-900 leading-relaxed">
          When joining two boards with a wood screw, drilling only a pilot hole is a critical error. The aggressive screw threads will bite into both the upper and lower boards. If the two boards are separated by even a hairline fraction of an inch, the threads bridge the gap and lock the pieces apart—a defect known as <em>screw jacking</em>. An unthreaded <strong>shank clearance hole</strong> in the top board allows the screw head to draw the joint fully tight.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-6">Standard US Wood Screw Dimensions &amp; Pilot Drill Reference (ASME B18.6.1)</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
